    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d92-a7">

                <pb facs="0172" n="88" sameAs="frus:frus1903/pg88"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Subinclosure 6.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Conger to Rear-Admiral Evans.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1903-08-04"><hi rendition="#italic">August 4,
                        1903</hi>.</date></dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">Mis., No. 1882.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your communication of the 30th ultimo, with extract from the report of the
                    commanding officer of the U. S. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Villalobos</hi>
                    concerning his recent visit to Nanchang, and the taotai’s objection thereto as
                    made to Consul-General Wilcox at Hankow.</p>
                <p>My letter of the 30th ultimo evidently crossed yours en poste. I beg to add to
                    that, however, that the matter will be duly brought to the attention of the
                    Chinese Government; but I will be pleased to have you point out to me the
                    provisions of the treaty which give our gunboats the right to go wherever they
                    please in the interior of the Empire, except on rivers leading to open
                    ports.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">E. H. Conger.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
